en_issues_count":0,"license":{"key":"mit","name":"MIT License","spdx_id":"MIT","url":"https://api.github.com/licenses/mit","node_id":"MDc6TGljZW5zZTEz"},"allow_forking":true,"is_template":false,"web_commit_signoff_required":false,"has_pull_requests":true,"pull_request_creation_policy":"all","topics":[],"visibility":"public","forks":0,"open_issues":0,"watchers":0,"default_branch":"main"}},"_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pulls/18"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pull/18"},"issue":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/issues/18"},"comments":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/issues/18/comments"},"review_comments":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pulls/18/comments"},"review_comment":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pulls/comments{/number}"},"commits":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pulls/18/commits"},"statuses":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/statuses/dd1f4cc624e621cb472757d642c988a210dea742"}},"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","auto_merge":null,"assignee":null,"active_lock_reason":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pulls/17","id":4967485899,"node_id":"PR_kwDOU-E3Y88AAAABKBXRyw","html_url":"https://github.com/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pull/17","diff_url":"https://github.com/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pull/17.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/pull/17.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/conjectures-io/conjectures-optimisation-float-kernels/issues/17","number":17,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"Score the full entry on a wider, private test set, behind GATE_FULL_SUITE","user":{"login":"rob-dendrite","id":289653679,"node_id":"U_kgDOEUPDrw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/289653679?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite","html_url":"https://github.com/rob-dendrite","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rob-dendrite/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## What and why\n\nThe full entry is timed on twelve fixed, public input classes: one exponent band, one `sum` length (1000) and one `bmm` shape (50×100×50). NaN, infinities and subnormal inputs are never timed, and the `mul` classes contain no zeros. A kernel can therefore win with a fast path whose slow fallback the benchmark never pays for.\n\nWith `GATE_FULL_SUITE` set, the full entry is instead timed on a fresh plan of cases drawn per run from a **suite**, a much wider test set. **Only the inputs change.** The ratio is the gate's legacy one (total fastest time over total fastest time), the paired round totals and admission are the legacy ones, and the verdict keeps its fields. The validator and the boards need no change. Without the setting nothing changes, and practice boards are unchanged either way.\n\n## What changes\n\n- **`benchmark_suite.py`**:\n  - loads and validates a suite: *groups* of value *families* with parameter ranges, work per kernel, and size distributions. Score weights are refused, because the score is total time over total time;\n  - draws a plan from the seed, with each group's cases per kernel plus one untimed raw-bits checksum case per kernel. A kernel's `work` is split evenly over its cases;\n  - **sizes each case to take about equal time on the shipped baseline.** Once a NaN, an infinity, a ±max or an overflow enters a `sum` or a dot product, the baseline's steps cost 30–100 times a clean one's. With equal work per case, those few cases took 85–90% of the total time and were bimodal in time. Each case's budget is now divided by its estimated cost, `1 + fallback_cost × slow_share`, computed from its drawn parameters, never from a timing: specials placement and kinds, a product overflowing on its own, a running sum overflowing over many terms (per band of term sizes, as a random walk or a line), and the accumulation length. A kind of input therefore counts by its number of cases, as the twelve legacy classes count about equally;\n  - sums it like the legacy classes. Per case, the fastest round of each side is taken; the ratio is total over total; the rounds are per-round totals. A cell (kernel × group) in `classes` is its cases' total over total.\n- **`lean/bench/BenchSuite.lean`** builds the operands from SplitMix64 counter streams:\n  - uniform or normal-like magnitudes;\n  - zeros, alone or in runs: `+0`, `-0`, signed, or leaked tiny values;\n  - subnormal blocks;\n  - specials (±∞, NaN payloads, ±max, subnormals, ±0) at a rate, on a stride, in bursts or at row edges;\n  - fractions reduced to bf16, fp16 or fewer bits;\n  - codebooks and per-block scales;\n  - for `add` and `mul`, a second operand tied to the first: equal, negated, cancelling, near, constant, identity, or offset by binades;\n  - `sum` lengths and `bmm` shapes drawn from wide ranges.\n\n  `MainFull` runs it when `BENCH_PLAN` is set.\n- **`gate.py`**: draws the plan, passes it in `BENCH_PLAN`, and requires every case's row and matching checksums.\n  - The verdict publishes per-cell times and ratios in `classes`, and the suite's version and file hash in `suite`. It never includes the plan or per-case data, so a private suite's contents stay private.\n  - The suite file's hash enters the verifier fingerprint.\n- **`suites/public-v2.json`**: the public suite (7 groups, 152 timed cases per run, 12 × `BENCH_PAIRS` work per kernel) for local tuning.\n- **`validator/tools/kings.py`** (read-only): writes each paid king's `Kernel.lean` and the exact source its verdict recorded as the champion it beat, then prints one gate command per king. This re-times the kings on the suite before switching, with no store writes.\n- **Docs**: BENCHMARKING (suite scoring and case sizing), MANUAL, OPERATIONS (switching a board to a private suite, re-timing the kings), `.env.example`.\n\n`validator/` is unchanged.\n\n## Checks run locally\n\n- `ruff check .` and `ruff format --check .`: clean.\n- `pytest -q` against a throwaway Postgres 17: 269 passed, 7 skipped. The new tests cover:\n  - plans: reproducibility, coverage, budgets, size spread, refusing broken suites (weights included);\n  - the slow-share estimate (specials, single and accumulated overflow), and slow cases getting proportionally fewer steps;\n  - gate scoring with a fake bench: the legacy total over total, legacy per-round totals, no plan or per-case data in the verdict, missing cases, checksum mismatches, practice slots ignoring the suite;\n  - the worker and the weight setter reaching the same decision from a suite verdict with the unchanged admission;\n  - the kings export: newest first, demoted ones skipped, each with the file it beat, including a since-demoted champion and the shipped incumbent.\n- **Real binaries.** Built with `lake build benchFull` for the shipped incumbent and three PROD-era kernels, and run end to end on legacy and suites with checksums agreeing:\n  - On the baseline, kernel time shares under the suite are add 0.22–0.29, mul 0.12–0.14, sum 0.19–0.26 and bmm 0.36–0.47, against legacy's 0.23 / 0.18 / 0.21 / 0.38.\n  - Without the sizing, a few poisoned cases took 85–90% of the time and the baseline scored 0.715 against itself. With it, the baseline scores 1.000 ± 0.003 at 5 rounds.\n  - The cost estimate is within 0.35–2.2× of the measured cost for 90% of about 1,100 cases. Under-estimates, the ones that would let a case dominate, are at most about 5×.\n  - A run's dependence on which cases it drew is about 5% for very uneven kernels and under 1% for even ones.\n- **Cost.** A baseline bench process takes 4.6–4.8 s on a suite, against 2.4–3.4 s on legacy, so about 1.5–2× the current benchmark.\n- **Not run:** the slow `-m slow` gate test and `selftest.py`, which need a bwrap sandbox; timing on the PROD gate host.\n\n## Deploy / operator steps\n\nNone while `GATE_FULL_SUITE` is unset. Merging rebuilds the pinned incumbent binaries once, because the bench sources change. To score the full board on a suite, see OPERATIONS.md, \"Scoring the full board on a private suite\":\n- keep the suite file outside the checkout, mode 0600;\n- raise `GATE_BUDGET_SECONDS` and `VERIFY_TOTAL_TIMEOUT`;\n- re-time the paid kings with `validator/tools/kings.py`, which also checks that the champion builds and benchmarks on the suite;\n- set `GATE_FULL_SUITE` and restart the gate worker. The kings, their ranks and their pay stay; later entries are timed against the champion on the suite.\n- Do not run `reverify.py` for the switch: it would requeue every accepted entry.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","created_at":"2026-10-22T12:38:57Z","updated_at":"2026-10-22T12:58:30Z","closed_at":"2026-10-22T12:58:29Z","merged_at":"2026-10-22T12:58:29Z","merge_commit_sha":"70d6da176d8ab5524ec2b44e81c6230162ec7362","assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[{"